{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/curseborne/1546424-selling-family-flavour","id":1546424,"title":"Selling Family Flavour","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Curseborne"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-11-26T08:34:55-08:00","id":1546424,"title":"Selling Family Flavour","content":"So something that's come up in a few threads is the relative lack of hard game mechanical differentiation between the Families. It doesn't matter if your Wild Forn is the Wolfman, a Deep One or a a ten foot cloud of roiling Elemental chaos, it just gives you +1 ETS (Enhancement that stacks) on Physical actions. It doesn't matter if you're a Bathorite or a Gaki, RAW you're equally incapable of perceiving ghosts. Your bargains with a member of the League of Hidden Crossroads, even if you sign a contract in blood, are not more binding than your bargains with a Battleground Angel (or for that matter your bargains with just any regular person).<br /> <br /> A lot of people have asked for dedicated subsystems for all this and I don't think that's on the cards. But I do think you can achieve a lot with just the stuff already baked into the game. Some of this I think is genuinely the intended playstyle. Some of it seems to go against the way I've seen the game run by actual devs on APs. So y'know, ymmv.<br /> <br /> 1) Be generous with Enhancements.<br /> <br /> A +1 Enhancement is pretty easy to come by and that in turn means it should be given out fairly freely. Remember you basically get an Enhancement for any kind of usable tool so it's really not a big deal.<br /> <br /> Similarly, be generous with letting shapeshifters have natural weapons. You don't want a situation where the Raptor turning into her Griffin form and mauling the enemy with her claws is mechanically less effective than her just using a knife. <br /> <br /> 2) Use Advantage Liberally<br /> <br /> Most of the time, Advantage is just 2 ETS. It's good but not back breaking.<br /> <br /> Effects that give out *fixed* advantage should be pretty rare. Power Advantage in combat, for example, is actively crushing because you both make your attacks stronger and penalise your opponent's defensive actions.<br /> <br /> But situational Advantage should be used pretty freely. Especially for interactions with mundane humans who really shouldn't be equipped to deal with the supernatural.<br /> <br /> 3) Use Altered Outcomes and Curse Dice Tricks<br /> <br /> I think a lot of the APs I've seen were run under the Ashcan assumptions that Wicked Success and Cruel Failure are a feature of all rolls but the manuscript rules seem to dial that back to make them just a thing for when magic is involved. But this also opens up space to make them more impactful when they do happen.<br /> <br /> Want that Hyde or Chimera to be able to pull off impossible feats of science? Want that contract your Crossroadser negotiated to actually have mystical backing? That seems to be exactly the kind of thing Altered Outcomes are for.<br /> <br /> I do think this is contrary to design intent; any kind of control over the Curse seems to be beyond the scope of the base game but, well, I don't agree with that.<br /> <br /> 4) Be Generous With &quot;Reinforcing Family Themes&quot;<br /> <br /> Having looked deeply into the &quot;okay but how do Gaki do Necromancy actually&quot; question I think the intent is that they just use their regular spells on ghosts. And in a lot of ways I'm cool with that but it does mean that Gaki aren't actually *better* at Necromancy than other Families and in some ways they're worse than Heirs, who get to cast Psychic spells for free.<br /> <br /> But the Storyguide can adjust this by making generous use of the &quot;reinforcing themes&quot; criterion for gaining CD. If a Gaki, for example, gets to claim a Curse Die for using Dire Command on a ghost instead of a mortal, they get to effectively discount the power as well as benefiting from their Family Motifs.<br /> <br /> A lot of Families have their theme strongly reinforced by spells that cost CD and if players are always hovering on 1-2 CD that's a problem. If they can claim back the CD they spend on their flavour powers it becomes much less of an issue.<br /> <br /> 5) Assume Supernatural Ability<br /> <br /> This game flat out doesn't work as written if you assume PCs have to have a specific game mechanical ability to do supernatural stuff. If that's the case then literally half the Hungry families can't feed at all.<br /> <br /> If a Vorare can suck out somebody's soul just by being a Vorare then I see no reason a member of the League of Hidden Crossroads can't drain somebody's soul as part of a contract.<br /> <br /> This does get awkward when you start giving players &quot;flavour&quot; abilities that overlap with spells but, well, that ship has already sailed.<br /> <br /> 6) Use Player Narrative Control<br /> <br /> Players can spend a Momentum to introduce narrative effects to a scene and there is zero reason that these effects can't be supernatural things the PC did. Players are also clearly intended to have a lot of leeway in how they narrate &quot;side&quot; scenes of, say, Hungry feeding. <br /> <br /> I will say that this approach isn't one I prefer because it introduces as artifical distinction between &quot;plot&quot; scenes and &quot;flavour&quot; scenes and the PCs might wind up working quite differently between them. But the option to use Momentum to have &quot;narrative&quot; effects work in mechanical scenes too alleviates that.<br /> <br /> 7) Embrace the Damnation Power Up<br /> <br /> There's a bit of ludonarrative dissonance with the way Damnations work (except for Sorcerers and maybe Outcasts). Narratively, your Damnation is the Curse taking over. It's the Creature or your Hunger or your Urges taking over and making you more of a monster and less of a person. Mechanically, the throes of Damnation are when your character is least powerful. You have 0CD so you can't cast spells, can't activate many of your Lineage abilities, never roll Wicked Successes or Cruel Failures. You're your closest to mortal when you're in Damnation.<br /> <br /> It seems like there might be a design intent that this be resolved by narrative effects and Storyguide narration. Mechanically your rampaging Lykan can't afford to activate half their combat spells but narratively it probably makes sense if they get some kind of bonus for wrecking things.<br /> <br /> You probably don't want to go as far as just giving everybody Fast Casting, but uncontrolled activation of spell effects or free activation of spell effects for the explicit purpose of furthering the Damnation seem like a good call. I think I'd probably also have *all* rolls in Damnation risk Altered Outcomes.<br /> <br /> Anyway, those are my thoughts on using the existing rules to plug what some of us see as &quot;gaps&quot; in the current system. Any other ideas?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}